WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.646 --> 00:00:04.646
(explosives firing)

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.760
- [Narrator] This March 3rd video shows

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.400
civilian vehicles being
fired on while fleeing

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.453
the occupied Ukrainian
village of Motyzhyn.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:18.360
Located outside of Kyiv between
Motyzhyn and Yasnohorodka,

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.266
a roughly four mile stretch
of road served as a crucial

00:00:21.266 --> 00:00:25.103
but perilous escape route
from incoming Russian forces.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.310
Local police said 37
civilians died here between

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.423
late February and the end of March.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.000
The Wall Street Journal
analyzed videos, photos,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.340
messages posted to social
media and weapon debris.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.480
We interviewed multiple
people in the village

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.350
and reviewed phone
calls between residents.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.600
We didn't account for all of the deaths,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.960
but we found evidence
from a few key incidents

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.790
that give a window into how
a four mile stretch of road

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.070
became one of the most
blatant examples in the war

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.240
of Russian soldiers attacking civilians

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.190
attempting to flee.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.310
One man who made it
through the road in March,

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.313
Yuriy Suprunenko recalled the horror.

00:01:05.313 --> 00:01:08.480
- (foreign language).

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.850
- [Narrator] There are three main routes

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.840
in and out of Motyzhyn.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.250
During the first week of the war,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.230
the two roads to the
north became the site of

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.510
many civilian killings
as they tried to flee

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.233
Russian forces pushing south.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.950
Many Motyzhyn residents turned
to a road running southeast

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.900
toward the village of Yasnohorodka

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.160
in hopes of getting to places
free of Russian troops.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.720
But this southern route would become

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.160
just as perilous throughout the month.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.810
The stretch of road closer
to Yasnohorodka is where

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.000
many civilians were attacked.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.170
On March 3rd a caravan of
three evacuating vehicles

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.087
were fired on here.

00:01:56.087 --> 00:01:59.087
(explosives firing)

00:02:01.999 --> 00:02:05.660
(explosives firing)

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.790
Weapons experts say
that the two projectiles

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.890
seen in these videos are likely mortar

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.120
and appear from the left of the frame

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.522
indicating that they were likely shot from

00:02:13.522 --> 00:02:17.390
the northeast side of the
road, just east of Motyzhyn,

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.420
which a driver in one of
these vehicles confirmed.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.450
In April we found debris
on the same stretch of road

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.860
that experts say is from an 82 millimeter,

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.959
or 120 millimeter mortar,

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:29.874
because of the shape of the tail fin

00:02:29.874 --> 00:02:32.283
and the markings seen here.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.750
These types of mortars
can be fired from roughly

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.300
two to five miles away.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.060
We found evidence of heavy
Russian military activity

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.730
in that range throughout the month.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:46.550
In a March 5th recording shared
with The Wall Street Journal

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.540
Yuriy Suprunenko, who was
still in Motyzhyn at the time

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.120
called in a Russian firing
location in this area

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.911
to a member of the volunteer
Ukrainian military.

00:02:55.911 --> 00:02:59.744
- [Yuriy] (foreign language).

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.530
- [Narrator] Just to the
west of the firing location

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.627
Suprunenko reported, we
can see Russian vehicles

00:03:13.627 --> 00:03:16.373
in a drone video from late March.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.830
In a forested area just
north of the Russian vehicles

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.170
in early April, The
Wall Street Journal saw

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.193
more evidence of Russian troops.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.840
A mass grave and military badges from

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.280
the 37th Guards Motor Rifle Brigade.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.300
Residents also said Russian troops

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.740
fired on cars from
positions close to the road.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.850
The Russian embassy in Washington DC

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.210
didn't respond to requests for comment.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:47.720
The Kremlin has said previously

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.583
it is not targeting civilians.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.050
On March 7th a message posted
in a Motyzhyn Telegram group

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.533
warned of a roadblock.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.080
That same day, a civilian
named Oleh Moskalenko

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.020
who was traveling towards Motyzhyn

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:04.770
was taken captive at a checkpoint

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.073
according to his daughter.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.720
His car was left on the road.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.800
Rocks, likely used to block the road

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.250
are visible in videos filmed
in the days that followed.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.150
Moskalenko's friends and family
used the Find My Phone tool

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.830
to track his phone.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:21.030
The last coordinates
point to a line of trees

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.690
along the road.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.270
Moskalenko's daughter said
that he was questioned about

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.300
being a member of the
Ukrainian forces and beaten.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.200
He was eventually released by the Russians

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.220
and is now recovering
in a German hospital.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.610
We can still see Oleh's car on the road in

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.513
a March 10th video filmed by evacuees.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.490
We also see another car abandoned nearby

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.293
suggesting more civilians
were captured, or fled.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.020
On March 16th a news
video shows a third car

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.010
that's been abandoned, a
damaged red station wagon

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.170
with a white cloth tied to a side mirror

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.310
and a sign in the windshield
that reads "Children"

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.933
in Russian and Ukrainian.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.020
Near the vehicles
Ukrainian soldiers discover

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:03.853
the dead body of a local man.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.050
Facebook messages reveal that
a woman driving in the car

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.140
was rescued and taken to
the hospital with injuries.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.463
We were unable to determine
whether she ultimately survived.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.900
What Yuriy Suprunenko describes
in the March 17th video

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.519
after his escape speaks
to the lasting toll of

00:05:21.519 --> 00:05:23.433
Russian brutality on the road.

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:27.886
- (foreign language).

